<InsertLayout
  title="Public-surface audit, worked example: our own site"
  description="The complete public-surface audit product, performed on toledotechnologies.com. 52 checks, 14 findings including one high, the full evidence, and the one finding that was withdrawn because it did not reproduce."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  heading="Worked example: our own site"
  subheading="The same 52 checks, run against toledotechnologies.com. Fourteen findings, one of them ours to be embarrassed about."
>
  <p>
    This is the <a href="/audit">public-surface audit</a> performed in full, on
    the only subject we can publish without asking anyone's permission: our own
    domain. Nothing is redacted and nothing is softened. The high finding is a
    real defect in our own mail configuration, published here because an audit
    product whose worked example is flattering is not evidence of anything.
  </p>

  <h2>The run</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Subject:</strong> <code>{RUN.subject}</code></li>
    <li><strong>Evaluated as of:</strong> {RUN.date}</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Checks defined:</strong> {RUN.checks} — every one appears exactly
      once in the output
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Findings:</strong> {RUN.findings} ({RUN.high} high, {RUN.medium} medium,
      {RUN.low} low)
    </li>
    <li><strong>Passed:</strong> {RUN.passed}</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Not established:</strong> {RUN.unknown} — no check failed to
      complete on this run
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Work done:</strong> {RUN.pages} pages retrieved across {RUN.requests}
      network requests, single-threaded, rate limited
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Evidence digest (SHA-256):</strong>
      <code class="break-all">{RUN.digest}</code>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Reproduce it before you read it</h2>
  <p>
    Five files, all published. The first is the whole method; the rest are this
    run.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="/audit/public_surface_check.py" download data-astro-reload
        >public_surface_check.py</a
      > — the checker. One file, Python 3.9+, standard library only. MIT licensed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a
        href="/audit/toledo-technologies-report.md"
        download
        data-astro-reload>toledo-technologies-report.md</a
      > — the report exactly as generated, all 52 checks.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a
        href="/audit/toledo-technologies-findings.json"
        download
        data-astro-reload>toledo-technologies-findings.json</a
      > — the same thing machine-readable.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a
        href="/audit/toledo-technologies-evidence.json"
        download
        data-astro-reload>toledo-technologies-evidence.json</a
      > — every captured DNS answer, HTTP response and certificate. About 700 KB.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a
        href="/audit/toledo-technologies-run.json"
        download
        data-astro-reload>toledo-technologies-run.json</a
      > — the crawl parameters this run used.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Run it against us yourself:</p>
  <pre><code>python3 public_surface_check.py toledotechnologies.com \
    --max-pages 30 --today 2026-09-11</code></pre>
  <p>
    Or re-derive this exact report from the captured bytes, with the network
    unplugged. Download the script, the report, the evidence and the run
    parameters into one directory, then:
  </p>
  <pre><code>mkdir evidence
cp toledo-technologies-evidence.json evidence/evidence.json
cp toledo-technologies-run.json      evidence/run.json

python3 public_surface_check.py toledotechnologies.com \
    --replay ./evidence --generated-at 2026-09-11T00:00:00Z \
    --md replay.md

diff replay.md toledo-technologies-report.md</code></pre>
  <p>
    That diff is empty. The replay walks the same page graph because the crawl
    parameters travel with the evidence — which is what
    <code>run.json</code> is for. An earlier version did not save them, a replay
    with a different page limit silently produced different findings, and the
    reproducibility claim was false until that was fixed.
  </p>

  <h2>High: our own DMARC policy does nothing</h2>
  <p>
    <code>mail.dmarc_policy</code>. The record exists and has existed for a
    while. It is set to monitor only.
  </p>
  <pre><code>v=DMARC1; p=none; rua=mailto:hello@toledotechnologies.com;pct=100; aspf=r; adkim=r</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>p=none</code> instructs receiving mail providers to do nothing when a
    message claiming to be from this domain fails authentication. Not quarantine
    it. Nothing. A stranger can send an invoice that appears to come from
    <code>toledotechnologies.com</code>, and it lands in the inbox looking
    exactly like a real one. Publishing DMARC at <code>p=none</code> and
    considering the matter handled is the single most common mail
    misconfiguration there is, and we were doing it.
  </p>
  <p>
    What makes this worth ranking high rather than shrugging at is that the
    expensive part is already done. Three other checks passed:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>mail.spf_all</code> — the SPF record ends in <code>-all</code>, a
      hard fail, not the <code>~all</code> most domains stop at.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>mail.spf_lookups</code> — it costs 4 of the 10 permitted DNS
      lookups, so it is nowhere near the limit that turns SPF into a permanent
      error.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>mail.dkim</code> — a selector is published and signing.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    So authentication works. Only the instruction to act on it is missing. This
    is the difference between a finding and a fix list, and it is the part a
    free scanner does not give you:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      Reports are already going to a monitored address, so the enumeration step
      is done. Read two to four weeks of aggregate reports and confirm every
      legitimate sending source passes alignment. Expect to find one — a form
      handler, a newsletter tool — that nobody remembered.
    </li>
    <li>
      Move to <code>p=quarantine; pct=25</code>. A quarter of failing mail goes
      to spam. If a legitimate sender was missed, this is where it surfaces, at
      a quarter of the blast radius.
    </li>
    <li>Raise to <code>pct=100</code> once a full reporting cycle is clean.</li>
    <li>
      Then <code>p=reject</code>. Add <code>sp=reject</code> at the same time, or
      every subdomain remains an open relay for the same forgery.
    </li>
    <li>
      Leave <code>aspf=r</code> and <code>adkim=r</code> alone. Relaxed
      alignment is correct here; tightening to strict before the reports prove
      it is how a working newsletter starts bouncing.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Four DNS edits over roughly six weeks, most of it waiting for reports rather
    than working. Nothing in that list costs money.
  </p>

  <h2>Medium: two</h2>

  <h3>Prices are published, but only to human readers</h3>
  <p>
    <code>claims.price</code>. Twenty distinct prices appear in the visible text
    across the site. Zero appear in structured data, despite 229 JSON-LD nodes
    being present across 30 pages — so the machinery is there, and the prices
    are not in it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The consequence is narrow and real: anything that reads pages rather than
    looking at them — a shopping surface, a directory, an assistant answering
    "what does this cost" — has to scrape the number out of prose or give up.
    Guessing wrong is worse than not knowing, because a wrong price is quoted
    with the same confidence as a right one.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fix is an <code>Offer</code> node on each page that names a price, with
    the same number that is visible on it. The check that would then apply is
    the one directly above it in the report: visible prices and structured-data
    prices must agree. They currently cannot disagree, because one side is
    empty.
  </p>

  <h3>Form fields with no programmatic label</h3>
  <p>
    <code>a11y.labels</code>. Five fields on <code>/care</code> — two selects, a
    textarea, a text input and an email input — resolve to no
    <code>&lt;label for&gt;</code>, no <code>aria-label</code> and no
    <code>title</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    This finding ships with its own limit attached, in the report, not as a
    footnote here: <strong
      >the parser does not detect a wrapping <code>&lt;label&gt;</code></strong
    >. It is a list to verify, not a verdict. That is how this check is
    delivered to a customer too — a check that can be wrong in a known way says
    so at the point it reports, or the whole report has to be treated as
    unreliable.
  </p>

  <h2>Low: eleven</h2>
  <p>
    Ranked low because each one is either cheap to fix and low consequence, or
    genuinely optional. A report that ranked all fourteen as urgent would be
    easier to sell and worth less.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong><code>ct.hostnames</code></strong> — nine hostnames under this
      domain appear in public Certificate Transparency logs, and all nine still
      resolve: the apex, <code>www</code>, and seven others.
      <br />
      The script ranks this low because a resolving hostname is not by itself a
      defect. What the list is for is the review it forces, and that is the
      whole argument for the product: Certificate Transparency is a public,
      permanent, searchable log of every hostname you have ever certificated,
      and it does not care which ones you meant to keep. A script can hand you
      the list. Going through it and deciding which entries should still be
      reachable — and telling the owner privately rather than publishing the
      conclusion — is the judgement being sold, and it is not something a
      scanner does for you.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>domain.dnssec</code></strong> — no DS record. Answers about
      this domain can be forged on the resolution path. One registrar toggle.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>domain.caa</code></strong> — no CAA record, so any public CA
      may issue for this name.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>http.headers</code></strong> — four of the five standard
      protection headers are present and correct. Content-Security-Policy is
      absent.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>content.security_txt</code></strong> — no RFC 9116 file, so
      someone who finds a problem has to guess where to report it. The usual
      guess is somewhere public.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>claims.policy_dates</code></strong> — no privacy or terms
      page carries a date it took effect, so none of them can be shown to have
      been in force on any particular day.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>mail.mta_sts</code></strong> and
      <strong><code>mail.tls_rpt</code></strong> — no MTA-STS policy and no TLS
      reporting, so inbound SMTP can be downgraded to plaintext and we would not
      know.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>a11y.headings</code></strong> — six pages with no
      <code>h1</code>, thirty with a skipped or repeated heading level. The home
      page has two <code>h1</code> elements.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>perf.render_blocking</code></strong> — thirty pages load one
      synchronous script in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>content.favicon</code></strong> — <code>/favicon.ico</code>
      returns 404. Icons are declared in the HTML, which most things honour, but
      some readers and chat previews ask for the root path first.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What passed</h2>
  <p>
    Thirty-eight checks, and listing them matters as much as listing the
    failures — a report that only says what is wrong gives you no way to tell a
    healthy surface from an unaudited one. The full list is in the report file.
    The ones that were not free:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>content.links_internal</code> — all 49 distinct internal link
      targets resolve. No broken links anywhere in 30 pages.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>content.sitemap_entries</code> — the sitemap index resolves through
      to 55 page URLs and all 40 sampled entries return success. Sitemaps
      listing dead URLs is one of the most common self-inflicted indexing bugs
      there is.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>content.titles</code> and <code>content.descriptions</code> — 30
      pages, 30 distinct titles, 30 distinct descriptions, none missing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>tls.names</code> — the apex and <code>www</code> each present a
      certificate naming themselves. See below.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>http.hsts</code> — <code>max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains;
      preload</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>http.cookies</code> — the home page sets no cookies at all on a
      plain GET, so there is no consent question to have.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>http.ttfb</code> — median 66 ms over three samples. One location, no
      browser; this is connection and server time, not page load.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>domain.locks</code> — <code>clientTransferProhibited</code> and
      <code>clientDeleteProhibited</code> are both set.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="withdrawn">The finding that was withdrawn</h2>
  <p>
    A draft of the checker reported this, as a medium:
  </p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>
      <em>tls.names — Certificate does not cover every hostname you use.
      Missing: www.toledotechnologies.com.</em>
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    It is false. The check connected to the apex, read that certificate's
    subject alternative names, and compared them against both hostname forms.
    The apex certificate names only the apex — because
    <code>www.toledotechnologies.com</code> presents its own certificate, issued
    separately, naming itself. Connecting to <code>www</code> directly shows it
    immediately.
  </p>
  <p>
    The finding was withdrawn. The check was rewritten to connect to each
    hostname and compare that hostname against the certificate
    <em>it</em> presents. It now passes, correctly, and the rewritten version is
    the one published above. The comment explaining why is left in the source.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is what the acceptance test on the <a href="/audit">product page</a> is
    for. A finding that does not reproduce is withdrawn; if more than three are
    withdrawn the audit is free. Publishing the one that was caught is cheaper
    than the alternative, which is a customer finding it instead and reasonably
    concluding the other thirteen are guesses too.
  </p>

  <h2>What this audit could not see</h2>
  <p>
    Stated because the value of the report depends on its edges being known:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Anything behind a login, any application logic, any database, any
      authenticated page. All 52 checks are outside-only. A clean report says
      nothing about them.
    </li>
    <li>
      Anything a browser does. No JavaScript was executed. A page that assembles
      itself client-side would be largely invisible to this, and a real Core Web
      Vitals measurement is a different instrument.
    </li>
    <li>
      Whether the DKIM selectors found are the only ones. Selectors are
      arbitrary strings; conventional names are probed, one was found, and
      absence would have been reported as not established rather than as a
      failure.
    </li>
    <li>
      Colour contrast, focus order, keyboard traps, and every other
      accessibility question that needs a rendered page. The six accessibility
      checks here are a defect list from static HTML and are not a conformance
      statement of any kind.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What this demonstrates, and what it does not</h2>
  <p>
    It demonstrates the method on a real subject, with the evidence published
    and the tooling public. It is not a client reference. Toledo Technologies
    LLC is the subject and the auditor, which is exactly why the interesting
    part of this page is the high finding and the withdrawal rather than the
    thirty-eight passes.
  </p>
  <p>
    One thing this page does not publish, on purpose. The evidence files are
    complete — every hostname, header and record is in them, because all of it
    is already public and withholding it would make the reproducibility claim a
    lie. What is not written up here is the <em>conclusion</em>: which specific
    entries on a list like that one deserve action, and why. That goes to the
    owner in writing and not onto a public page. It applies to us here exactly
    as it would to a customer — a report is written for the person who can fix
    it, not for whoever reads it next.
  </p>
  <p>
    The other runnable samples are at <a href="/proofs">/proofs</a>, and they
    use invented fixture data. This page does not — it is a real run against a
    real domain. The audit product itself, with its price, boundary and
    acceptance test, is at <a href="/audit">/audit</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Toledo Technologies LLC is the contracting supplier, and it is AI-operated:
    correspondence, the audit itself and the written report are run by AI, with
    the company accountable for delivery. This run and this page were produced
    the same way. How that works is set out on the
    <a href="/about">about page</a>.
  </p>
</InsertLayout>
